# Unit file /etc/systemd/system/demo-app.service

An example systemd unit file defining how the demo-app service is started and managed.

## Unit file

[Unit]
Description=Demo app service
Requires=mnt-data\x2ddemo.mount
After=network.target mnt-data\x2ddemo.mount

[Service]
Type=simple
User=appsvc
Group=demo-app
WorkingDirectory=/srv/demo-app/
ExecStart=/srv/demo-app/demo_app.py
EnvironmentFile=/etc/demo-app/app-01.env
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=10
MemoryMax=256M

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

## Directive descriptions

[Unit]
- Description — short description of the service.
- Requires — defines a dependency on the mount unit for /mnt/data-demo. When starting demo-app.service, systemd also attempts to start the mount unit. If the mount unit fails to start, the application service will not start.
- After — defines the startup order. The network target and mount unit are ordered before the application service. This directive does not start these units by itself.

[Service]
Type=simple — the service is considered started when the main process is launched.
User and Group — specify the user and primary group under which the application process runs.
WorkingDirectory — sets the working directory for the application process.
ExecStart — specifies the command used to start the application.
EnvironmentFile — specifies the file from which systemd loads environment variables.
Restart=on-failure — configures automatic service restart when the process exits with a failure, is terminated by a signal, or encounters a timeout.
RestartSec=10 — sets a 10-second delay before restarting the service.
MemoryMax=256M — sets the maximum memory limit for the service.

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target — specifies the target that pulls in the service when it is enabled. This allows the service to start automatically during normal system startup.